    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Port Arthur mold damage claims rarely start with mold. They start with water: a roof opened by a storm, a burst supply line, an AC drain that overflowed for a weekend, or a flood that kept a house wet while owners were evacuated. In Southeast Texas, where humidity stays high for much of the year, mold can grow on wet drywall, insulation, and wood within days. Insurance treats mold as secondary damage, and how it is handled depends on the original cause of the water and the specific mold language in your policy. This page keeps to general principles, because mold coverage varies widely from one policy to another.</p>

<h2>Why Mold Is Such a Problem After Water Losses Here</h2>
<p>Port Arthur’s climate combines heat, humidity, and heavy rain. After Hurricane Harvey in 2017, when the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service</a> reports more than 64,000 homes flooded in Jefferson County, many homes sat wet for days or weeks because owners could not get back in, power was out, or contractors were booked. Mold followed. The same pattern happens on a smaller scale after any leak that is not dried quickly.</p>
<p>Housing construction plays a role too. Pier-and-beam homes can trap moisture under the floor, and older homes may have materials and wall cavities that dry slowly. Slab homes with a hidden plumbing leak can stay wet under flooring for a long time before anyone notices.</p>

<h2>How Policies Typically Treat Mold</h2>
<p>There is no single rule. Common approaches include:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Policy approach</th><th>What it usually means</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>General mold exclusion</td><td>Mold is excluded unless it results from a covered peril</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mold resulting from a covered water loss</td><td>Covered, often only if the water event was sudden and reported promptly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mold sublimit or endorsement</td><td>Coverage capped at a set dollar amount, sometimes including testing and remediation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Exclusion for long-term leaks or humidity</td><td>Mold from repeated seepage, condensation, or high humidity is not covered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood policy (NFIP)</td><td>Mold caused by flood may be covered; mold the owner could have prevented after the water receded may not be</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Your declarations page and endorsements list will show whether you have a mold sublimit. If you do, check whether testing, remediation, and rebuilding all count against it.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/adjuster-inspecting-ceiling-damage-with-homeowners.webp" alt="Adjuster and homeowners inspecting ceiling damage for moisture and mold" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Mold often shows up first where water was trapped: ceilings, wall bottoms, and behind cabinets.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>The Original Water Source Decides Most Mold Claims</h2>
<p>Because mold coverage usually follows the water, the first task is establishing what caused the water and when.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Storm-created roof opening:</strong> mold may be part of a <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind</a> or <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA</a> claim, subject to the policy’s mold terms.</li>
<li><strong>Sudden plumbing or appliance leak:</strong> mold may follow a covered <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claim</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Rising water or surge:</strong> mold is handled under the <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood policy</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Slow leak, condensation, or chronic humidity:</strong> often excluded.</li>
</ul>

[[cta]]

<h2>Steps That Help a Mold Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop the water and start drying.</strong> Delay is one of the most common reasons mold claims are reduced.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph before cleanup.</strong> Capture the water source, visible mold, and affected materials, with dates.</li>
<li><strong>Report promptly.</strong> Tell the insurer about both the water event and the mold.</li>
<li><strong>Keep drying records.</strong> Moisture logs and equipment invoices show you acted quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Use qualified professionals.</strong> Texas regulates mold assessment and remediation, and larger projects may require licensed professionals. Check the requirements before work starts.</li>
<li><strong>Document things you could not control.</strong> Evacuation orders, power outages, and contractor shortages after a hurricane can explain delays in drying.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to save.</p>

<h2>Where Mold Claims Run Into Trouble</h2>
<ul>
<li>The insurer attributes mold to a long-term leak or humidity rather than the reported event.</li>
<li>The policy’s mold sublimit is much lower than the cost of remediation and rebuilding.</li>
<li>The estimate covers cleaning but not removal and replacement of affected drywall, insulation, or cabinets.</li>
<li>Contents damaged by mold, such as upholstered furniture and clothing, are left out.</li>
<li>Additional living expenses while the home is unlivable during remediation are not addressed.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Mold After Evacuations and Long Power Outages</h2>
<p>Port Arthur residents know the pattern: a storm approaches, an evacuation order goes out, and by the time owners return, the house has been closed up without air conditioning for days. NOAA records that during Hurricane Laura in 2020, more than 60 percent of Jefferson County lost power at the storm’s peak (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=918105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>). A small roof leak in a hot, sealed house can produce far more mold than the same leak would in a cooled home. If you were kept away by an evacuation order, a road closure, or a lack of power, write down those dates. They can help explain to the insurer why drying did not begin sooner and why the mold spread as far as it did.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Can Help With Mold Claims</h2>
<p>A public adjuster represents the policyholder. On a mold claim, that may include tracing and documenting the original water source, reviewing the mold provisions and sublimits in your policy, coordinating with the assessor’s and remediator’s reports, preparing a rebuild estimate, and presenting the full claim to the insurer. We do not perform mold testing or remediation, and we cannot guarantee that mold will be covered. Port Arthur Public Adjuster is a service of [[company]], TDI license #[[license]]. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>If a mold claim was denied, see our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page. For health concerns, talk to a medical professional; for legal questions, consult a Texas attorney. We serve Port Arthur, Jefferson County, and surrounding Southeast Texas communities including <a href="/service-areas/beaumont/">Beaumont</a> and Groves.</p>
